<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The vessel's gates ~ one assembly for every gate a node stands

The sovereign sync route and the floor's oracle route stand on the same gate (`@lararium/mesh`
`face-gate`). What differs between them is the ROOT their bundle derives under and the CONSUMER that seats
their sessions, never the readers. `vesselGate` is the one place those readers meet the gate.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-readers`>>

!! What every gate of one vessel reads

* ''The admit reader'' holds the root of the gate's own Nexus alone (`gateHeldRoot`), read fresh at every
admit. A gate at Nexus A never judges an admit by Nexus B's board.
* ''The shadow ring'' is the vessel's one D4 reading (`gate-shadow-ring`): a device the fleet root set
aside holds no same-operator seat, whatever it proves (house ruling "A device set aside").
* ''The grant ring'' answers the PersonaGroup grant this vessel holds for a face (`gate-rings`).
* ''The sorter'' reads this Nexus's posture alone (`socket-sorter`), and the walker's arms ship SHUT.
* ''The withdrawal memory'' outlives a restart where the composer hands the sealed store
(`gate-memory-rows`); a floor gate keeps it in memory.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-bundle-and-the-table`>>

!! The bundle and the table come from outside

The gate's bundle comes from the composer, derived under the tier's own root door: a sovereign route's from
the custody root, a floor route's from the floor key. The session table comes from the route's own
consumer, so no frame reaches a consumer before the gate's verdict left. A refusal's cause goes to this
vessel's own log through `onRefuse`; the wire never carries it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/edges`>>

